{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The Emim formerly</b> etc. You might suppose that this is the land of the Rephaim that I gave to Abraham, because the Emim — who are Rephaim — dwelt in it. But it is not that one, for those Rephaim I dispossessed on account of the children of Lot and settled others in their place. The Rephaim were so called because they were reckoned like the Anakim, who are called Rephaim on account of everyone who sees them — his hands go slack (מתרפות). Emim — because their terror (אימתם) is cast upon all creatures. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 2:11). And likewise it is written (Deuteronomy 2:20), <b>It too is reckoned a land of Rephaim</b>, because the Rephaim dwelt in it formerly, but it is not the one that I gave to Abraham.\n\nAnd I am astonished at the Rav's words: why does Scripture explain to us concerning the land of Ammon and Moab that it is not the land of the Rephaim that He gave to Abraham? If the intent is to say that it is not fit for Israel and is not part of the land that HaShem swore to their fathers to give them — but Scripture says only <b>for to the children of Lot I have given it as a possession</b> (Deuteronomy 2:19), to say that even though it was part of the inheritance of Abraham, the Holy One, blessed be He, gave it to the children of Lot for Abraham's sake, just as Rashi himself wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 2:5), that He gave two portions to the children of Lot as reward for having gone with him to Egypt and for keeping silent about what he said concerning his wife, \"she is my sister.\" Moreover, the Rav himself wrote concerning Esav: \"Ten nations I gave to Abraham; seven for you, and Keni and Kenizi and Kadmoni — they are Ammon and Moab and Mount Seir; one of them to Esav and two to the children of Lot.\" If so, the land of Ammon and Moab and Mount Seir is part of Abraham's inheritance in any event, so why does the Holy One, blessed be He, say to Moses not to suppose that it is the land of the Rephaim of Abraham? What difference is there between Keni and Kenizi and Kadmoni on the one hand and the Rephaim on the other? Furthermore, <b>Rephaim dwelt therein formerly</b> (Deuteronomy 2:20) — is it to say that it is theirs and they are the ones who dwelt in it before any man, as in <b>Of old You founded the earth</b> (Psalms 102:26)?\n\nBut the meaning of the verses is the opposite of the Rav's words. For because the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: \"Do not harass Moab, for I have given Ar as a possession to the children of Lot,\" Scripture relates that the land is fit for the seed of Abraham, were it not that it was given to the children of Lot, and it says that in that land there dwelt from of old a great and lofty people like the Anakim, and therefore the Moabites called them Emim, because of the greatness of the terror that they cast upon those who saw them. And behold, HaShem performed a miracle for the children of Lot for the honor of Abraham, and they overcame them and dispossessed them, and it is not fitting to take from them by force the land that HaShem gave them by an act of miracle.\n\n<b>They are reckoned</b> — an expression of reckoning and significance, as in <b>despised, and we reckoned him not</b> (Isaiah 53:3), <b>against whom silver is not reckoned</b> (Isaiah 13:17), <b>for with what is he reckoned?</b> (Isaiah 2:22), and so many others, and in the language of the Sages: \"a person of significance (אדם חשוב).\"\n\nAnd this is why Scripture juxtaposes to this (Deuteronomy 2:12): <b>And in Seir the Horim dwelt formerly</b> — it says that for the same reason that He mentioned concerning Moab, He will also restrain them from the land of Seir, for formerly it too belonged to the Horim, and it is part of the gift of Abraham, for the Hori is the Hivvi and he is of the children of Canaan, as it is stated (Genesis 36:2): \"Esav took his wives from the daughters of Canaan\" etc., \"and Oholivamah the daughter of Anah, the daughter of Tziv'on the Hivvi,\" and Tziv'on was of the children of Seir the Hori, the inhabitants of the land (ibid. v. 20). And likewise He mentions in every place, in the inheritance of Israel, the Hivvi together with the Canaanite among the seven nations, for he is counted as the sixth son among the children of Canaan (Genesis 10:17). And so you shall see in the war of Amraphel, who waged war against the king of Sodom and Gomorrah and Admah and Tzevoyim and Zoar — who are kings of Canaan — and they smote the Rephaim in Ashterot Karnayim and the Hori in their mountain of Seir (Genesis 14:5), for the Hori and the Rephaim belonged to the kings of Canaan. And it is possible that he was called חוי (Hivvi) because חוי means a serpent (נחש) by way of allusion, and he was called חורי (Hori) on account of חור פתן (Isaiah 11:8), for the names preserve the sense and interchange the words, like Zerach and Tzochar (Genesis 46:10) from וצמר צחר (Ezekiel 27:18), אתנות צחרות (Judges 5:10), and so they do for praise — they would call Jacob, which is a term of deception or crookedness, yet they call him Yeshurun, from the language of integrity and uprightness. And behold, it says that the Horim, who belong to Abraham, dwelt in Seir formerly, and the children of Esav, who are the seed of Abraham, dispossessed them and dwelt in their place by an act of miracle — for they were a numerous people in their land and Esav came to sojourn there and prevailed over them, just as Israel did to the rest of the nations of Abraham that HaShem gave them through His great might. And behold, HaShem bequeathed all those peoples to the seed of Abraham: one of them to Esav and the rest to Israel, who is the firstborn son, and it is not fitting to take by force from Esav that which Elohim dispossessed for them, for HaShem will be wrathful against whoever takes by force an inheritance that He bequeathed, just as He will be wrathful against whoever takes by force from Israel the land after He bequeaths it to them. As it says (Jeremiah 12:14): \"Thus says HaShem concerning all My evil neighbors who touch the inheritance that I bequeathed to My people\" etc.\n\nAnd this is the meaning of <b>as Israel did</b>, which He mentions here even though it has not yet been done. And He says further concerning the children of Ammon, that it is the land of the Rephaim (Deuteronomy 2:20), for it too was originally that of the Rephaim, and the Ammonites called them Zamzumim, from the language of <b>do not bring forth his plan (זממו)</b> (Psalms 140:9) — except that it is doubled, meaning a people from whom nothing they plan to do is withheld."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:59.170879072Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}